# Analyzer severity for the test suites only.
#
# The file has two halves, split by the separator below because they answer different questions. Above it
# are rules enabled repo-wide in /.globalconfig that the suites turn back OFF. Below it are rules that
# arrive with AnalysisMode=Recommended — the mode the suites run at, the same as the rest of the repository
# — that the suites DECLINE. /.globalconfig is divided the same way for the same reason.
#
# Neither half is a blanket "tests are exempt". Every entry is turned off on its own, with its own reason,
# and this file is a path-scoped .editorconfig precisely so that turning one back on is a one-line change.

[*.cs]

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Rules from /.globalconfig turned back off for the suites.
#
# All four are about the lifetime and blocking behaviour of objects inside a long-running process. A test
# method is not one: it runs once, on a fixture the runner tears down, in a host process that exits.
# Enforcing them here was measured at 414 findings against 39 in the code that ships — the ratio alone says
# the rules are matching the shape of test code rather than a defect in it. The remaining ~20 rules from
# /.globalconfig stay in force here.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# 328 of 335 findings. The objects are per-test harnesses, view-models and headless windows whose scope IS
# the test method; the runner disposes the fixture and the process exits after the run.
dotnet_diagnostic.CA2000.severity = none

# Every finding is an ignored TryParse on a literal the test itself wrote. A parse that unexpectedly failed
# would leave the out value at default and fail the assertion that follows it — the test is the check.
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1806.severity = none

# Blocking is the point: a test body waits for the operation it is asserting on. The rule's concern is a
# thread-pool starvation that a single-threaded test host cannot suffer.
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1849.severity = none

# Fires on `using` + a task in the same scope, which in these fixtures is awaited before the scope ends.
# The rule cannot see that, and the alternative is restructuring tests to satisfy a flow analysis.
dotnet_diagnostic.CA2025.severity = none

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Rules DECLINED out of AnalysisMode=Recommended, for the suites.
#
# A rule belongs here only if its findings across the suites are either not defects, or not fixable without
# a change bigger than the rule is worth. A rule whose findings are merely INCONVENIENT gets fixed instead,
# and most of what the mode brought in was fixed on exactly that ground. The culture rules it adds
# (CA1304/CA1305/CA1310/CA1311) are the clearest case: they closed a gap the suites had no cover for at all,
# so every one of their findings was fixed and none of them appears below.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# CA1707 (identifiers should not contain underscores) is off. It is the largest finding count the mode
# brings in by a wide margin — several times the next rule down, and more than every other rule here
# put together.
#
# Almost all of it is one thing: the test method name. These suites name a test
# `Verb_Condition_Expectation` (AddProgram_IsRefused_InsideALockedBlock), which is not a stylistic accident
# but the reason a failing test is readable in a runner's flat list of names, where the underscores are the
# only structure a reader gets. NUnit's own documentation, and every convention this repository already
# follows, spell them this way. Renaming them into Pascal case would remove the separation and change
# nothing about what the test does.
#
# The remainder is the assembly and namespace names — `safe_project_tests` and its peers. Those names are
# what CLAUDE.md, README and CI name in their `dotnet test` lines, and what the coverage slices are keyed
# by under artifacts/coverage/raw/<suite>/. This is the same finding, on the same ground, that
# applications/, utilities/ and shared/ each already decline: the repository's assembly names carry
# underscores deliberately, and renaming one to satisfy a spelling rule would leave the name disagreeing
# with everything that invokes it.
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1707.severity = none

# CA1861 (prefer a 'static readonly' field over a constant array argument) is off. It is the second-largest
# count the mode brings in, and the shape it fires on is the assertion itself: more than half the findings
# are the expected value inside `Assert.That(x, Is.EqualTo(new[] { ... }))`, and most of the rest are the
# input literal a test writes one line above the call it is testing.
#
# Satisfying it would hoist each of those literals into a field at the top of the fixture, away from the
# assertion that reads it. That is a worse test: the value a case is about stops being visible at the case,
# and a reader has to resolve a name to see what is being compared. The rule is aimed at a hot call site
# re-allocating an array per call; a test method runs once.
#
# Three findings were NOT test data — the `IndexOfAny(new[] { '+', '/' })` calls in ArchRuleHelpers, which
# really are called per type across a whole assembly scan. Turning this rule off does not release them:
# CA1870 fires on the same three lines, stays on, and asks for the better fix (a cached SearchValues) than
# the one CA1861 would have asked for.
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1861.severity = none

# CA1826 (do not use Enumerable methods on indexable collections) is off. Read this entry together with the
# rules it does NOT cover: CA1829, CA1845, CA1847, CA1860, CA1865 and CA1870 are the same performance family,
# they all fire here too, and every one of them was FIXED rather than listed. So this is not "the suites
# decline performance rules" — it is one rule, declined on what it asks for.
#
# What it asks for is `.First()` → `[0]` and `.Last()` → `[^1]` across arrange-and-assert code: the receiver
# is `project.Groups`, `element.Children`, a catalogue list — collections a test builds, reads once, and
# discards. The enumerator allocation the rule removes is charged against a method the runner calls once.
#
# The part that decides it is the four `LastOrDefault()` sites, where the rewrite is not neutral but worse.
# Each one hands a possibly-null Activity to an assertion helper (AssertRefusedScope, AssertAllChannels)
# whose job is to report "no span was recorded" as a named failure. `[^1]` turns that into an
# ArgumentOutOfRangeException from inside the arrange step, which says nothing about what the test was
# checking. A rule that trades a diagnostic for an allocation is measuring the wrong thing here.
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1826.severity = none

# CA1859 (use concrete types for improved performance) is off. Note first what it is NOT: every project that
# ships satisfies this rule with no suppression anywhere, so this is a statement about the shape of test code
# and not a rule the repository has quietly given up on.
#
# What it asks for here is the reverse of what each site says on purpose. Two shapes cover all of it:
#
#   - A private fixture helper that returns `IReadOnlyList<string>` / `IReadOnlyCollection<string>` — a list
#     of offences an assertion then reads. CA1859 wants `List<string>` / `HashSet<string>`. That hands the
#     caller a mutable collection where the signature currently promises a snapshot, which is a weaker
#     contract bought with an interface dispatch that a helper called once per test cannot notice.
#
#   - A local typed as the abstraction the production code sees: `IValueConstraint required =
#     RequiredFieldConstraint.For(…)`, `ICatalog RequireCatalog() => new BuiltInCatalog()`,
#     `ILibraryBlockSource library = …`. Here the interface IS the assertion. RequiredFieldExemplarTests
#     proves the constraint behaves correctly when reached through IValueConstraint; retyping the local to
#     the concrete class does not speed the test up, it deletes what the test was checking.
#
# The arch suite adds a third reason of its own. Its findings would put ArchUnitNET's fluent-builder
# conjunction types (GivenTypesConjunctionWithDescription, TypesShouldConjunction) into helper signatures,
# pinning the fixtures to that library's internal builder shape — in a suite whose entire job is to assert
# that everything else depends on abstractions rather than implementations.
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1859.severity = none
